            <div xml:id="d88">

                <pb facs="0227" n="155" xml:id="pg155"/>

                <head>No. 88.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Morton to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-06-27"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 27, 1882</date>.
                        (Received July 13.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 191.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I regret to have to inform the Department
                    that the pork bill, of which a copy was sent with my dispatch No. 146, of March
                    31, 1882, was defeated in the Senate a few days ago.</p>
                <p>This result was quite unexpected. The bill had the unequivocal support of the
                    government, and of the senate committee, to which it had been referred. The
                    chairman of this committee, Senator Wurtz, the eminent chemist, and dean of-the
                    Academy of Medicine, reported the bill in the strongest language possible,
                    showing, by conclusive proofs and arguments, that the trichinæ, wherever it
                    existed, could not resist the double process of salting and cooking, and that,
                    with a proper inspection, the introduction into France of our fully cured pork
                    could not be attended with any danger or inconvenience. Mr. Tirard, the minister
                    of commerce, advocated the passage of the bill on the same ground. The
                    opposition, which came mainly from the hog-raising departments, contended that
                    if there were no danger in allowing the free introduction of American meats into
                    France, the government could remove the decree of prohibition without any law,
                    and succeeded in defeating the bill by a majority of four votes.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * * *</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>LEVI P. MORTON.</signed></closer>
            </div>
